xt":"To determine the shape described by the equation \\( r = c \\) in cylindrical coordinates \\((r, \\theta, z)\\), we need to understand the meaning of each coordinate in cylindrical coordinates. In cylindrical coordinates, \\( r \\) represents the radial distance from the \\( z \\)-axis, \\( \\theta \\) represents the angle in the \\( xy \\)-plane from the positive \\( x \\)-axis, and \\( z \\) represents the height along the \\( z \\)-axis.\n\nThe equation \\( r = c \\) means that the radial distance from the \\( z \\)-axis is always \\( c \\), where \\( c \\) is a constant. This describes a set of points that are always a fixed distance \\( c \\) from the \\( z \\)-axis. In other words, it is a cylindrical surface whose radius is \\( c \\) and whose axis is the \\( z \\)-axis. The value of \\( \\theta \\) and \\( z \\) can vary freely, which means that we can move along the \\( z \\)-axis and rotate around the \\( z \\)-axis, but the distance from the \\( z \\)-axis remains constant at \\( c \\).\n\nTherefore, the shape described by the equation \\( r = c \\) is a cylinder.\n\nThe correct answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\text{E}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:14"}
